From: =?UTF-8?q?Bedirhan=20Gen=C3=A7aslan?=
 <b2210356065@cs.hacettepe.edu.tr>
Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 01:21:07 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 25/71] Keep the prompt cache warm while idle

Chat-wire providers key their prompt cache off the literal message prefix, so
the entry lives on a short server-side TTL with no key to refresh it by name.
Any pause longer than that TTL makes the next request re-bill the whole prefix
uncached, which measured as the largest single source of cache-miss cost in a
normal session. Replay the last request's exact wire body with the output
capped at one token every two minutes of idleness, five times at most, since
beyond that the replays cost about as much as the miss they prevent.

The replay lives below the session and is handed only a transport, a provider,
an auth provider, and the body the endpoint actually sent. It holds no
reference to history, the rollout, the event channel, or token accounting, so
it cannot reach anything it would corrupt. Its stream is dropped unread:
response headers arrive after prefill, which is the moment the entry is
refreshed. The Responses wire is excluded because it has no output cap and its
cache is named by a server-owned key that a replay cannot refresh.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.6 <noreply@anthropic.com>
